    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 032345\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n445 PM MST Thu Sep 3 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- A few showers and storms are possible across south-central Arizona \nlate this afternoon into early this evening.\n\n- Some tropical moisture arriving into the region through early \nFriday will increase rain chances across the foothills and higher \nterrain areas east of Phoenix before chances shift westward into \nsouthwest Arizona and southeast California this weekend.\n\n- Temperatures will remain in a near to slightly below normal \ncategory through this weekend, resulting in widespread minor Heat \nRisk and localized areas of moderate HeatRisk. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nLatest objective analysis reveals a broad subtropical ridge \nextending from the southern Plains into the southeastern U.S. while \na broad trough is encompassing the Pacific Northwest. This overall \npattern configuration is resulting in southwesterly flow across the \nregion, especially in the mid to upper-levels, which has advected \nconsiderably drier air aloft. Meanwhile, moisture advection from \nHurricane Marie, located southwest of the tip of the Baja Peninsula \nwill result in a surge of low-level moisture heading into this \nafternoon and tonight with PWATs likely to exceed 1.5 inches across \nmuch of south-central and southeast AZ while much drier air with \nPWATs of less than one inch is expected to be confined across \nsouthwest AZ and southeast CA. The latest Hi-Res guidance for later \nthis afternoon and this evening does show some isolated showers and \nstorms popping up near some of the terrain features such as the \nTable Top and Estrella Mountains in south-central AZ, however, the \nvery dry air above 600 mb will likely limit the residence time of \nany activity. Some gusty winds and locally heavy rainfall will be \nthe main threats with any of these storms. Heading into tonight and \nearly Friday morning, guidance is showing another round of showers \nand storms to develop across portions of southeast AZ and move \nnorthward, affecting the foothills and higher terrain areas east of \nPhoenix where the best moisture axis is likely to set up. With the \nactivity overnight into early Friday morning, some locally heavy \nrainfall that could lead to localized flooding will be the main \nthreat. Quieter conditions are anticipated starting late Friday \nmorning as drier air filtering in from the west moves in, dropping \nPWATs under 1.3\" with little to no instability and thus eliminating \nany rain/storm chances through the rest of the day.\n\nDue to the presence of the troughing across the Pacific Northwest, \nafternoon high temperatures this afternoon and Friday will be within \na few degrees of the daily normals as readings top out in the upper \n90s to low 100s across much of the lower desert communities. The \nassociated height falls from the trough will also cause some \nafternoon/early evening breeziness across portions of southeast CA \nand southwest AZ with peak gusts upwards of 20-30 mph, potentially \nupwards of 40 mph in the typical wind prone areas of far southwest \nImperial County. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nGlobal models are coming into somewhat better agreement with the \npattern evolution this weekend. Both the ECMWF and GFS show high \npressure strengthening over the southern Plains with the western \nperiphery of the ridge extending over E AZ and NM. This will cause \nthe subtropical jet to shift wwd beginning Saturday, setting up \nbetter moisture advection over southeast CA and southwest AZ. NBM \nPoPs are still not overly impressive, mainly in the 20-30% range on \nSaturday, however the GEFS/GFS indicates a final slug of moisture \nfrom Marie being pulled into southeast CA on Sunday with PWATs \nrising above 2.0\". Therefore we will have to monitor for heavier \nrainfall potential in portions of SE California including Joshua \nTree NP on Sunday. Elsewhere, across southcentral AZ, rain chances \nlook to be meager due to more extensive cloud cover and very low \ninstability. The latest WPC QPF field shows a local minimum in \nrainfall totals (<0.10\") in southcentral AZ including the Phoenix \nMetro through this weekend. By early next week, there are large \ndiscrepancies in the ridge/trough positioning which will complicate \nthe precipitation forecast, but there will still be adequate \nmoisture across our region which will keep low end PoPs in place \nacross eastern and southeastern AZ Monday and Tuesday with \npotentially better precipitation chances arriving Wednesday.\n\nThe temperature forecast this weekend into early next week remains \nlargely unchanged, although there will likely be a gradual increase \nfrom near normal temperatures on Saturday to slightly above normal \ntemperatures Sunday into early next week as ridging strengthens over \nthe region. Depending on how strong the ridge gets, NBM temps could \nclimb a few more degrees in subsequent runs and it is not out of the \nquestion that we could see lower desert highs approaching 110 \ndegrees as indicated by the NBM 75th percentile on Monday and \nTuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2345Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe threat for isold TSRA this evening, then scattered SHRA early \nFriday morning are both weather concerns along with uncertainty in \nwinds through Friday. Under periods of mid/high clouds decks, have \nmaintained a prob30 mention of TS this evening as activity may pop \nover the Estrellas, however confidence is somewhat better that \nscattered SHRA impact the area late tonight and near sunrise Friday \nthough even this activity may remain east of terminals. Otherwise, \nwinds may be highly variable through the period only briefly \nobtaining a SW component this evening before reverting to E/SE. \nSHRA may create erratic directions at times, and gusty 150v230 winds \nmay prevail early Friday afternoon before slowly becoming SW.  \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nOccasional late afternoon/early evening gusty winds will be the main \nweather issue through Friday evening under clear skies. SW gusts 20-\n25kt will be common at KBLH late afternoon while KIPL should \nexperience the most pronounced gusts through the evening. Speeds \nwill relax overnight and early morning where periods of nearly calm \nconditions will exist. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nIsolated to widely scattered showers and storms will be possible in \nsouthcentral AZ later today through Friday. Rain chances will expand \nwestward through this weekend, becoming focused over the western \ndistricts Saturday and Sunday. The best chances for wetting rainfall \nwill be over the higher terrain areas of eastern and southeastern \nAZ. Afternoon minimum humidities will range from 7-15% across \nsoutheast CA and between 20-35% for the remainder of the region \nthrough Friday and will likely increase above 25% areawide this \nweekend. Overnight recoveries will range from poor across the \nwestern deserts and good to excellent across the eastern districts. \nDaytime winds will remain elevated out of the south to southwest, \nwith the strongest gusts in Southeast CA and along the Lower \nColorado River Valley peaking between 20-30 mph during the \nafternoon/early evening.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock/Salerno\n",
